<h1>7.5 Monitoring caches and choosing technologies</h1>
<p class="lead">A cache you cannot measure is a cache you cannot trust. This last lesson covers what to monitor, shows a live dashboard of this app, compares other caching technologies, and ends with a summary of the whole course.</p>

<h2>What to measure</h2>
<div class="card">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Why</th><th>Warning sign</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><b>Hit ratio</b> (per cache, per key group)</td><td>The main measure of usefulness (lesson 1.1)</td><td>Dropping after a deploy: key or TTL bug</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Latency</b> of hits, misses and the source (p50, p99)</td><td>Shows whether the cache really saves time</td><td>Redis p99 rising: overload, big keys, network</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Evictions</b></td><td>Entries removed for space (lessons 1.4, 5.4)</td><td>Constant evictions: cache too small</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Memory and key count</b></td><td>Capacity planning</td><td>Steady growth: keys without TTL</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Errors and timeouts</b></td><td>The cache is a dependency that can fail (lesson 4.2)</td><td>Any increase, since it means fallbacks to the database</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Database load</b></td><td>The cache's real job is protecting it</td><td>Spikes in step with expiries: avalanche or stampede (7.1, 7.3)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h2>How to collect it in .NET</h2>
<ul>
    <li><b>IMemoryCache:</b> <code>TrackStatistics = true</code> and <code>GetCurrentStatistics()</code> (used since lesson 1.2). .NET also publishes them as metrics.</li>
    <li><b>Your own metrics:</b> <code>System.Diagnostics.Metrics</code>. This app has a meter named <code>Caching.Academy</code> with a counter <code>caching_academy.cache_events</code>, tagged by event type and level. Every event in every log on this site increments it.</li>
    <li><b>Watch it live:</b> <code>dotnet-counters monitor -n Caching.Core --counters Caching.Academy</code>, or export with OpenTelemetry to Prometheus/Grafana, Azure Monitor or any other backend.</li>
    <li><b>Redis:</b> <code>INFO</code> (hits, misses, memory, evictions, lesson 5.4), <code>SLOWLOG</code>, <code>LATENCY</code>, and your provider's dashboards.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>private static readonly Meter Meter = new("Caching.Academy");
private static readonly Counter&lt;long&gt; EventCounter =
    Meter.CreateCounter&lt;long&gt;("caching_academy.cache_events");

EventCounter.Add(1, new("type", type), new("level", level));</code></pre>

