<p class="muted">Use demos on other pages and come back: the numbers grow. Note how Hit and Miss events relate to the hit ratios.</p>

<h2>Other caching technologies</h2>
<div class="card">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Technology</th><th>What it is</th><th>When to consider it</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><b>Memcached</b></td><td>The original distributed memory cache: simple strings with TTL, multi-threaded, no persistence, no data structures. Scales by sharding on the client side.</td><td>Very simple, very large key-value caching. Most teams now choose Redis for its features.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Redis / Valkey</b></td><td>Data structure server (Level 5). Valkey is the open-source fork maintained by the Linux Foundation.</td><td>The default choice for distributed caching.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Garnet</b></td><td>Microsoft Research's Redis-compatible cache server, written in C#.</td><td>High throughput on .NET infrastructure. Works with StackExchange.Redis.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Dragonfly, KeyDB</b></td><td>Multi-threaded Redis-compatible servers.</td><td>Very high load on a single machine.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>NCache</b></td><td>A .NET-native distributed cache with read-through/write-through providers, SQL dependency invalidation and an <code>IDistributedCache</code> implementation.</td><td>.NET shops wanting cache-side loaders and Windows-friendly operations.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Hazelcast, Apache Ignite</b></td><td>In-memory data grids: distributed caches with compute, queries and near caches.</td><td>Large clusters needing more than key-value caching, often in Java-heavy environments.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>EF Core second-level cache</b></td><td>Libraries such as <code>EFCoreSecondLevelCacheInterceptor</code> cache query results by intercepting EF Core commands and invalidate them when the involved tables change.</td><td>Many repeated read queries in an EF Core app, with little code change. Less control than explicit caching.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>CDN, Varnish, Nginx</b></td><td>HTTP caches in front of your app (Level 2).</td><td>Public, anonymous, cacheable HTTP responses and static files.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Database-side</b></td><td>Buffer pools, materialized views, read replicas.</td><td>Sometimes the right answer is a faster query or a precomputed table, not a cache.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p>An example of the EF Core second-level cache, to compare with the explicit caching you wrote in this course:</p>
<pre><code>services.AddEFSecondLevelCache(options =&gt;
    options.UseMemoryCacheProvider().CacheAllQueries(CacheExpirationMode.Absolute, TimeSpan.FromMinutes(5)));

var products = await context.Products.Where(p =&gt; p.Price &gt; 100).Cacheable().ToListAsync();</code></pre>

<h2>The whole course on one card</h2>
<div class="card">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Remember</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><b>1. Fundamentals</b></td><td>A cache is a copy. Hit ratio decides everything. Always set expiration and size limits.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>2. HTTP</b></td><td><code>Cache-Control</code> for browsers and CDNs, ETags for cheap revalidation, output caching for server-side responses, versioned URLs for static files.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>3. Strategies</b></td><td>Cache-aside + delete on write is the default. Read-through, write-through, write-behind and refresh-ahead each solve a specific problem.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>4. Distributed</b></td><td>Several servers need a shared cache. Serialize compactly, fail open, and use HybridCache for L1 + L2.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>5. Redis</b></td><td>Use the right data type. Atomic commands solve counters, rate limits and leaderboards. Pub/Sub broadcasts, locks need tokens and safe expiry.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>6. Invalidation</b></td><td>TTL as a safety net, explicit invalidation for freshness, versioning or checks to beat races. Caches are eventually consistent.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>7. At scale</b></td><td>Single flight against stampedes, null caching and Bloom filters against penetration, jitter against avalanches, L1 against hot keys, and metrics for everything.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<div class="callout tip">
    <strong>Course complete</strong>
    You have gone from "what is a cache?" to the problems large systems solve every day. Revisit any lesson, read its code, and change the numbers (TTLs, sizes, delays) in the services to see how behavior changes. That is the best way to make it stick.
</div>

